// shader-differential — NAMED REGRESSION for the scalar-broadcast defect
// (t_b6f2a2a4).  Written because neither this project nor the Vita Cg
// compiler had a fixture for it: both were protected by general machinery,
// which is not the same as a test that fails when someone removes it.
//
// THE DEFECT: a SCALAR operand used under a MULTI-LANE write mask must
// broadcast lane 0.  A scalar literal lands in the constant block as
// {c,0,0,0}, so under the identity swizzle every lane past x reads ZERO:
//   ours       MUL R.xy, R, {6,0,0,0}      <- lane y multiplies by 0
//   reference  MUL R.xy, R, {6,...}.x      <- broadcast
// It compiles clean and renders wrong, and .x-only uses are immune, which
// is why it survived every shader that happened to use one lane.
//
// EACH CHANNEL ISOLATES ONE SCALAR SOURCE KIND, and all three read through
// lane Y — the lane that breaks.  If the broadcast is lost, that channel
// collapses to frac(0) = 0 while the others may survive, so a failure says
// WHICH kind regressed rather than just that something did:
//   R  scalar LITERAL   under a 2-lane mask
//   G  scalar UNIFORM   under a 2-lane mask
//   B  COMPUTED scalar  under a 2-lane mask
//
// Input is deliberately NON-FLAT (uv varies per pixel): a broadcast that is
// present in the encoding but wrong in the swizzle reads identically in a
// disassembly listing, so this has to be judged on pixels, not on a listing.
// Reviewed cross-project with the vita-cg-compiler team, who make the same
// point about their own ISA.

void main(float2 uv : TEXCOORD0,
          uniform float4 u_scale,
          out float4 color : COLOR)
{
    float2 lit  = uv * 6.0;             // scalar literal  -> both lanes
    float2 unif = uv * u_scale.x;       // scalar uniform  -> both lanes
    float  prod = uv.x + uv.y;          // computed scalar
    float2 comp = uv * prod;            // computed scalar -> both lanes

    color = float4(frac(lit).y, frac(unif).y, frac(comp).y, 1.0);
}
